    {
      "id": 197931,
      "name": "jffdougan",
      "username": "jffdougan",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/jffdougan/{size}/1882_2.png",
      "created_at": "2017-05-11T13:29:35.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Trajector\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/trajector/48/1914_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Trajector:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Ha! US Patent 1 seems pretty cute. It's definitely much more steampunky and linear than my prototype. We hit different time-travel archetypes!Just goes to show that for every pair of similar ideas, the concepts can diverge quite substantially. <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/slight_smile.png?v=12\" title=\":slight_smile:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":slight_smile:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>In case you weren't previously familiar with them, Cheapass Games' design philosophy was to only sell you the unique things you needed, and they were generally strong on theme while scattershot on mechanical tightness/balance. But, Kill Doctor Lucky has been continuously in print &amp; available for roughly 20 years, and One False Step For Man remain one of my favorite titles out of their catalog. (Hey, Paul - if you guys are reading this thread, ping James Earnest about whether he'd be interested in y'all bringing it back into print as a DHM or maybe Fabled Nexus title?)</p>",
      "post_number": 7,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:25:44.821Z",
      "reply_count": 0,
      "reply_to_post_number": null,
